> other message you'll see that ignorance of the material is not allowed as a 
> defence.  How can you prove your innocence when "I didn't know it was 
> illegal/didn't know it even existed" is disallowed as a defence?
>  
> Peter.

Since he didn't point it out, perhaps I could mention that Michael Hamel
is, in fact, a legal type himself. What he was pointing out, I believe,
was that 1) The police have some discretion about whether to bring
charges, and if you told a credible story about the Bambi tape (by
showing them that you had just rented it from a video outlet for
example) they wouldn't be likely to charge you. and 2) that even if they
did charge you, the purpose of having a jury, and a judge for that
matter, at a trial is to ensure that the law is *appropriately* applied,
and they can listen to your explaination  to determine your innocence.

This is not, of course to say, that the police or the laws are perfect.
They're not. They're just not perfectly bad.
