I am hoping members of this list might be able to help me with a
problem I have been poking away at, on and off, since June.  I am
a staff writer for the Canadian edition of Reader's Digest and we
have been trying to determine whether there is enough material to
warrant an article on the conflict between free speech and
political correctness on campus.  To wit:  has academic freedom
been compromised enough, at enough Canadian universities, to
justify raising an alarm.

What first caught my attention was a proposed speech code
(masquerading as an anti-harassment code) at Dalhousie.  After
criticism from a handful of brave faculty, the code was sent back
to the drawing board, but it may yet re-emerge.

The most egregious examples my research turned up -- Canizzo at
ROM/UofT, and Klatt and Ratcliffe at Western -- are a bit dated. 
Although almost all Canadian schools have some statement on
sexual harassment, most of these are fairly bland.  Only a few
go to the Draconian lengths proposed -- but not passed -- at Dal.

Some faculty and students have complained to me of an atmosphere
of intimidation that means certain courses don't get offered,
certain arguments go unvoiced.

I am interested in hearing your views as to whether there is a
big problem here.  And if so, can you supply specifics.  I will
be grateful for any replies.  (And don't feel you have to waste
band width replying to the whole group.  You can reply to me at
pdonham@fox.nstn.ns.ca)  Many thanks.


