I followed this debate on comp.infosystems.gopher fairly closely.
He is a brief account of the issues as I saw them.

A sysadmin at Oregon State had a gopher server with data consisting
of thousands of files whose names were digit strings.  It was made
known to people who would have interest in the data, but not 
advertised or "registered" with the gopher people at Minnesota.
The sysadmin had no objection to people on the net accessing his
data if they were interested in it.

Several (I think at least three) automated gopher indexers (e.g.
veronica) found his gopher and started indexing it.  According to
the sysadmin this could take a half an hour during which use of
his computer was severely impacted.  He contacted indexers and asked
them to stop.  According to the sysadmin they did not do so in a
timely fashion (on the order of a week, I think).  It is possible
they were not able to do so quickly, but the sysadmin seemed to
believe that at least some of the indexers were uncooperative.

The sysadmin posted an angry message to the group
comp.infosystems.gopher expressing his feelings about gopher indexers.
He also made the argument that indexers had no right to use his CPU
without his permission.  A number of people jumped on him (quit
unfairly in my opinion) saying he should not be trying to hoard his
data.  He responded that he had no objection to people accessing his
data, but he wanted the indexers to stop because 1) they were
continuing to impede his work and 2) the indexes produced (being only
numeric strings) were useless.  The sysadmin made several apparently
unsuccessful efforts to block indexing including moving his server to
a new port on the same machine.  One of the indexers who had stopped
indexing found the new port and started indexing again in the belief
that this was a "new server".  The sysadmin was clearly frustrated
could perhaps be characterized as intemperate in his posts.

Responding posters made the argument that having a gopher server
implicitly gave permission to anyone to use his CPU in whatever way
gopher supported.  Others made the argument that permission was
implicit in the registration of his gopher server with the Univ of
Minn.  The sysadmin responded that he had *not* registered it, that
one of the indexers had "registered it for him".

This discussion has lead to a mechanism, supported by at least the 
Veronica indexers, by which a gopher maintainer can mark a directory
(or the whole server) not to be indexed.


