leaf that they are protecting the victims of abuse,  and b) politicians
both internal and external who will use the groups claiming offense to
advance their purposes, and c) moralists who will enforce the moral
codes that can no longer be enforced in their own names as a way of
protecting the alleged victims.

>Finally, when a university droid censors newsgroups, and this event gets
>net.coverage, potential sponsors and students considering this university
>get to consider the censorship too..

The university droid is going to be doing the censoring at the DEMAND
of the sponsors and students.

>                             .. while the actual impact is limited,
>I'm sure the real university leaders will start to look down to such
>newsworthy blunders...

When the actual impact of the failure to censor is charges of racism,
sexism, *ism, and lawsuits for civil rights violations, which  will
be costly and damage image even if won, the real university leaders
will be only too glad to have some faceless administrator ( who they
can of course denounce as a gutless bureaucrat devoid of principles )
spare them further difficulties.

As long as people who feel offended by expression consider it their right to
demand sanctions on the source of whatever gave them offense, and as long
as those seeking to repress free expression are more active and vocal than
those who favor it, then censorship is very likely to occur.  It may,
however, be invisible, because it will occur when an individual decides
that he or she cannot risk exposing their ideas to public  scrutiny.  The
irony here is that truly false and vile ideas will flourish in the dark.

On the subject of things "in the dark", I like having pornography on
the Internet, and believe there are a lot more people who like it than
are offended by it, but so long as those who claim offense are more
vocal, censorship remains a very real concern.
