>
>Feminist literature can be found in libraries, and I presume, offices of
>feminist studies departments.  However, unlike the bible, each volume would 
>to be read to check whether it was unquestionably objectionable.  Borderline
>cases could be submitted to the new authority for a decision.

Unfortunately the feminists and bible-bashers (who, according to a civil- 
liberties group which made submissions on the act, were the main force 
behind it) have conveniently excluded any of their own material from being 
offensive.

Knowing what's in the new law makes the Canterbury Uni position a bit more 
understandable (although I still disagree with it completely).  While I 
don't want to turn this into an imminent death of the net predicted lament, 
the internet is a rather fragile institution over here.  There's just one 
main backbone going to universities and a few companies and 
govt.departments, and there's no official funding for it - it's all paid for 
by universities and the like from computer centre budgets or whatever.  All 
it takes is something like the alt.tasteless crossposts to rec.pets.cats a 
while back followed by a complaint from a self-appointed guardian of public 
morals for an administrator to panic and take drastic self-censorship 
steps.  At $5K fine per non-PC posting a single days news can eat the entire 
years network access budget, and many administrators would rather have a 
highly limited feed than none at all (and a criminal record to boot).

The NZ Internal Affairs dept. has been approached over this (for example 
some people suggested supplying them with a newsfeed in hardcopy format and 
creating a two-year backlog at the end of the first day), but their comment 
was that they basically weren't interested in checking everything (of course 
you're still guilty if you get caught with anything "illegal").  However 
this was before the new law came into effect, I don't know what their 
position is now.

There is, however, one glimmer of hope:  One comment I made during the 
discussion was:

>>(3) Without limiting the power of the Classification Office to classify a 
>>    publication as a restricted publication, a publication that would 
>>    otherwise be classified as objectionable may be classified as a 
>>    restricted publication in order that the publication may be made 
>>    available to particular persons or classes of persons for educational, 
>>    scientific, literary, artistic, or technical purposes.
>
>Hmm, "technical purposes":  "Yes your honour, I keep 2GB of white noise on 
>tapes for use in testing the sound quality of my speakers.  Encryption? 
>What's that?" :-)

As I mentioned before since the people who wrote the act are obviously 
utterly clueless when it comes to computers, the act says absolutely nothing 
about encryption.  The only provision is that the soundtrack of a film may 
have to be translated into English, but nothing else.  In fact if you had 
the resources and without that ^$%^&@! automatically-guilty clause you could 
probably bog any court case down indefinitely with arguments about 
representation of information and use various information theory and 
philosophy-based arguments to prove that either the law or their case as it 
pertains to computer data is a load of rubbish.

However all this does is provide privacy for personal data and 
communications - it still doesn't help mitigate the threat of possible news 
censorship... the only alternative is for anyone who wants an unlimited 
newsfeed to bring it in in encrypted form from the US, which is 
prohibitively expensive.

Oh yes, I don't speak for the Uni of Auckland, and YMMV on the above 
comments. 

