In case anyone's interested, here are a few relevant sections of the new law 
(complete with my totally unbiased:-) comments), from a discussion in 
nz.general a few weeks ago...

>  "Publication" means--
>[...]
>  (c) Any paper or other thing--
>[...]
>    (ii) On which is recorded or stored any information that, by the use of
>         any computer or other electronic device, is capable of being 
>         reproduced or shown as any word, statement, sign, or representation.

This shows the (typical) utter cluelessness of the people who drafted the 
law.  There is no "normal use" clause here - it's possible to interpret data 
in any way you choose if you want to.  There's no way that a jury consisting 
of people who can't set the timers on their VCR's are going to be able to 
differentiate between decoding a JPEG file and "decoding" an Excel 
spreadsheet to get, in both cases, a politically-incorrect image.

>    3. Meaning of "objectionable"--(1) For the purposes of this Act, 
>  a publication is objectionable if it describes, depicts, expresses, 
>  or otherwise deals with matters of sex, horror, crime, cruelty, or 
>  violence in such a manner that the availability of the publication 
>  is likely to be injurious to the public good. 
>
>  [long list of policitically incorrect topics covering virtually everything
>   imaginable]
>
>    4. Whether publication objectionable a matter of expert judgement--
>  The question whether or not a publication is objectionable is a matter 
>  for the expert judgement of the person or body authorised or required, 
>  by or pursuant to this Act, to determine it, and evidence as to, or 
>  proof of, any of the matters or particulars that the person or body is 
>  required to consider in determining that question is not essential to 
>  its determination. 

This means that even if the "person or authorised body" can't provide any 
proof or evidence showing that a "publication" is illegal under the act, 
they can still rule that it is "injurious to the public good".  It basically 
allows whoever gets to make the judgement to enfore their morals on everyone 
else.

And now the really scary part:

>Section 121. Offense to possess objectionable publication---
>
>(1) Subject to subsections 4 & 5 of this section, every person commits an 
>    offense against the Act who, without lawful authority or excuse, has in 
>    that person's possession an objectionable publication.
>(2) Every person who commits an offense against subsection 1 of 
>    this section is liable to a fine not exceeding, ---
>        (a) In the case on an individual, $2000:
>        (b) In the case of a body corporate, $5000.
>        [also jail terms which are listed somewhere else]
>(3) It shall be *no* defence to a charge under subsection 1 of this section 
>    that the defendant had no knowledge or no reasonable cause to believe 
>    that the publication to which the charge relates was objectionable.

Note that (3) provides for, in effect, imprisonment without trial.  Once any 
sort of non-PC material is in your hands, you can't defend yourself.  In 
effect you go from arrest straight to sentencing without any cute niceties 
like a trial by jury. 

One person commented:

>Fortunately there should be ample scope for this act to destroy itself. eg. 
