Unfortunately the feminists and bible-bashers (who, according to a civil- 
liberties group which made submissions on the act, were the main force 
behind it) have conveniently excluded any of their own material from being 
offensive.

Knowing what's in the new law makes the Canterbury Uni position a bit more 
understandable (although I still disagree with it completely).  While I 
don't want to turn this into an imminent death of the net predicted lament, 
the internet is a rather fragile institution over here.  There's just one 
main backbone going to universities and a few companies and 
govt.departments, and there's no official funding for it - it's all paid for 
by universities and the like from computer centre budgets or whatever.  All 
it takes is something like the alt.tasteless crossposts to rec.pets.cats a 
while back followed by a complaint from a self-appointed guardian of public 
morals for an administrator to panic and take drastic self-censorship 
steps.  At $5K fine per non-PC posting a single days news can eat the entire 
