Ah. Here we get on to the numerous and interesting differences in philosophy
and practice between the U.S and New Zealand approaches to law. Although the
systems look the same, with judges, lawyers, police, and so on, they are about
as similar as Macs and Windows PCs when you get down to it. (I'm a Mac
programmer, sorry if this doesn't mean much to you). There are deep-seated
differences. The key one here is that our judges are not in any way political
appointees. Nor are our prosecutors. No-one votes for them. Hence, they have no
axes to grind and no public reputation to maintain, other than a general one of
impartial justice. Politics and playing to the public gallery have no place in
a New Zealand courtroom, and there is a strong ethical convention against any
manifestation of them.

Imagine the differences this generates. For one thing, we are more willing to
trust our police and judiciary with the sort of discretions that laws of strict
liability, because they are above the day-to-day workings of politics. The
history of how all this came about is fascinating and mostly happened after the
American Revolution - hence the U.S didn't acquire it.

> 	From the sections posted here, it seems that this law is a great 
> tool to persecute gays and lesbians. Or does that just "not happen" in NZ?

No, it doesn't. The police just don't have a political agenda. Individual
police might, but the media reaction whenever there is any suggestion that this
might be happening is quite powerful. Its just not done.

> 	 The basic argument above seems to be based on selective
> enforcement mollifying the literal law to common sense (whatever that
> is) application. But the dangers of relying on that should be obvious
> (thought too frequently they aren't). I'd argue that it's not
> re-assuring to talk of human factors in applying overbroad laws, but
> rather the reverse. Having a situation where someone is subject to
> criminal penalties even though they had no reasonable way to know they
> were violating the law, and apparently no practical defense once
> charged, seems rife with potential for political abuse and repression.

Right. Thats the risk we run. We have quite a few laws that work this way. Its
a balancing act between the costs of trying to produce a workable and just
definition of an offence - which can be *enormous* as people employ time and
energy to trying to get around them - like the drink-driving laws - and the
risk of using strict liability, eliminating all of the defence arguments based
on intent or state of mind, and creating the potential for abuse. Given the way
our system works, the balance point is naturally different from in the U.S.

