This is one of my two major worries about the new act.  For example our current
police minister is pretty heavy on the "law and order" issue.  He's also better
know for his frequent public homophobic rantings than for reducing the crime
figures, which is ostensibly what his job is.
 
As an example of where this can lead, at the start of this year he made some
comments about cracking down on activities in the red light district here. Not
long afterwards there were huge police raids (something like 50-100 policemen
on a little backstreet place with maybe 10 rooms) on several of the strip clubs
there.  Although the area is not the most salubrious place to be at night, it's
probably at least as safe as anywhere else in the central city..... the only
thing the raids netted, AFAIK, were a few asian women working there illegally.
There was no real reason for these raids (the police have far better things to
devote their manpower to than hassling a few strip club owners) apart from "The      
boss said do it", and yet they were still carried out.
 
All it takes is a comment from the police minister about "there's a bit too
much in the way of smutty videos around" for something similar to happen there.
Even assuming that common sense prevails and nothing happens, headlines of
"University of Foo in porn bust" (in the case of "computer porn") will lead to
instant self-censorship by people wishing to avoid a similar situation
themselves.
 
This leads to my second concern:  Since noone really knows whether what they're
doing is wrong or not until charges are brought against them, it will lead to
"voluntary" self-censorship to reduce the chance that someone, somehow, might
accidentally see something they don't quite agree with. Again, as an example
take the action of the U of C which, according to the message which started
this thread, was influenced by the new act.  It's easy to say "It's OK, common
sense will prevail", but the two counterexamples I've quoted show that,
frequently, common sense doesn't prevail, especially if the new law is used as
a weapon in the fight.
 
I have this nasty feeling I'll be posting an "I told you so" message about the
new law in action by the middle of next year....
 
Peter.
