>Whatever the poster answers the newsposting software sends the message
>all over the place. I from UW's in Warwick UK to Washington US.  People
>periodically complain/enquire about this problem -- and are told "well,
>it is the way it works".

Well, that is the way it works. You will notice, however, that the
posting does not wind up in another hierarchy or even in a different
group. It is in the same group. It is the fault of the joint admins for
selecting non-unique newsgroup names. Syracuse University and Stanford
had the same problem: a raft of su.* newsgroups. If Oregon State used
osu.*, they would undoubtedly clash with Ohio State. Get your news
admins to use a different hierarchy than uw. 

>a) as a poster -- what about my right to restrict my posting to a 
>   geographical domain?  

There is no mechanism to do this. Hint: "Distribution" is not a way to
restrict anything. Any site can be part of any distribution they want to
be, even if that means a US site is getting Australian distributions.
Major news relays get many distributions, and it isn't uncommon for
minor sites to get distributions that are of interest to their users.

Distribution is no different than Newsgroups. Any site can get a
newsgroup, any site can get a distribution. The fact that they get that
distribution makes them part of that distribution.

>   Apparently I don't have that right, so why
>   should a poster expect their article to be restricted to particular  
>   groups? 

Because there is a way to do that.

>b) given that whatever a poster replies to the distribution prompt,
>   the post may well get sent far afield, why put out a dire warning
>   about how much the process is going to cost the community as a 
>   whole?

Because, properly configured, putting a "uw" distribution (if there is
such a beast) on an article will limit the spread to sites that want
"uw" articles. Properly configured, a "uw" distributed post will cost
the net a lot less than a "world" or null distributed post. Even if the
only sites that get "uw" distributions are those who are University of
W*, that is a lot less than "world".

(Most likely, once a "uw" posting gets loose, it will go around the
world, but that is not the poster's fault and not the local admin's
fault. That certainly isn't an excuse to refuse to try to limit it by
using a blank distribution.)


