All this stuff about the U of C censoring news takes me back a while.

Four years ago I was responsible for introducing netnews onto the campus
backbone at the University of Canterbury (it had been available in the cosc
department for sometime earlier). We discussed censorship then, and as I was
responsible I said that I couldn't censor on the grounds that I was incompetent
to decide what to censor, and further, that there was noone around who was (I
happen to believe that this statement still applies ...) Of course, the sheer
impracticality of the idea is obvious too, once one considers crossposts.

Anyway, as far as I can see the same defence I conjured up then still applies.
It's a defence by the sheer impossibility of prosecution. 

Think on it: The data exists on computers all over the university. Presumably
the law has to choose between prosecuting the individuals managing the computers
(rather a lot of people), and the institution. 

Can you imagine the state prosecuting a university? There is no way that the
university can stop the material entering the site (every telephone is a
potential method of data entry). On the other hand, can you imagine prosecuting
all the individuals: if we all confessed (which we can all do, whether we read
it or not, we have it on our news disks). Who to prosecute? The director, the
system manager, the news manager, the downstream site, the upstream site? All
the readers (we all have access to laser printers, so are probably in violation
of the law ...)?

This law is an ass! Like many other laws, it can be made to be seen to be an 
ass by ignoring it and letting them prosecute. If even 10% of the people who
are probably in violation of the law (in the sense that they could disseminate
the contents of certain newgroups) confessed it would clog the system and make
a very large public statement on the stupidity involved.

I said it then, and I'll say it again. Let them (whoever the faceless they are)
prosecute and prosecute and prosecute.

In the event that anyone at the UofC reads this, I'm sorry I wasn't there to
argue against this (again). The new law hasn't changed my opinion.

Bryan Lawrence

(Not the standard disclaimer: nothing I have said implies that I believe
that these newsgroups contain anything worth defending! All I'm saying is that
this law is an ass, and it's impossible to restrict information, even
information that we don't like)


