}Neither of these things happen here, to the best of my knowledge. Our
}drunk-driving rules are strict liability - the offence is being tested by a
}particular procedure as having a certain blood-alcohol level. No excuses,
}though God knows enough people try various things...

The abuses hinge on the definition of 'drunk driving'.  Drunk driving
is frequently defined as having the requisite BAC and being in control
of a motor vehicle.  If you are in the car with the keys, you are
considered to be in control of the motor vehicle-- even if you are
asleep in the back seat.  The same sort of finagling has been used to
convict "drunk drivers" who were attempting to drive cars with no
engine.  Abuse of other related laws is common-- the "open container"
laws are occasionally used to convict someone who has a 6-pack of beer
with one can missing.

How do your drunk driving laws handle the case where someone was made
drunk without their knowledge?  Do they get a drunk driving conviction
(which, at least in the US, can cause a lot of problems) and a
suspended sentence?

I don't doubt that these things are rare in NZ-- at least compared to
the US-- but I think that by becoming complacent you are in for a rude
shock when someone powerful finds a reason to start abusing these
laws.  And the pornography law posted here is, IMO,  written explicitly to be
abused.
