Matthew T. Russotto <russotto@eng.umd.edu> wrote:
>My point was actually exactly the opposite-- the copyright law could
>be interpreted to forbid re-posting of Usenet articles, or the posting
>of electronic mail by the recipient.  But I don't think (regardless of
>what the netiquette documents say) that either is wrong.  And if anyone
>expects me to behave as if they are, they're going to have to bring out
>the copyright law and use it against me in court.

Well, I don't feel a strong aversion to reposting Usenet articles
within Usenet.  I do, however, oppose those folks who copy Usenet
postings to other media (trade magaizines, books, etc.) without 
requesting the author's permission.

Posting email without the author's permission, on the other hand, is 
taboo to me.  I'm not concerned with copyright, slander, or any other 
legal issues; I'm concerned with common courtesy.  

I believe that posting to Usenet gives an *expectation* of publicity.
Posting a message to a mailing list, or uploading a file to a BBS or
FTP archive, gives a similar expectation of publicity.  The very na-
ture of person-to-person email, on the other hand, carries an implied 
expectation of confidentiality.  If I wanted everyone to hear me, I'd 
post, right?  If someone sends me email, I have to believe that, for 
whatever reason, they didn't want to broadcast/publicize the material 
in question.  Common courtesy dictates (to me, anyway) that I should 
respect their expectation of confidentiality in most situations.  I
can think of very few situations in which a verbatim reposting of
private email would be appropriate.  Paraphrases, on the other hand,
are acceptable to me; in most cases, I don't see anything wrong with 
"An email correspondent informs me that..."  IF the information itself
would reveal the source.

Is it *legally* permissible to publicize private email?  Probably.
Is it *ethically* permissible?  That's a question for each individual;
I have to say that, in most cases, I don't support such action.

--Wes

