>J. S. Greenfield <greeny@top.cis.syr.edu> wrote:
>>"Suppose some sends me an unsolicited message, demonstrating what a jerk they
>>are, and then says I can't show it to anybody.  Am I legally prohibited from
>>making the text of the message public."
>...
>>effect.  Sue for damages?  The work didn't have any commercial value in the
>>first place, so the author didn't suffer financial damages.
>>
>>It would seem to me that even if such publication were prohibited by
>>copyright law, there is not going to be any effective remedy under
>>copyright law.
>
>Well, if I ever you send you a letter that demonstrates that I am a jerk,
>and that you can't show it to anybody, I'll be sure to register the
>copyright.  Then I don't have to prove actual financial damages.  I'll
>get at least the minimum statutory dmages ($500 nowadays, isn't it?),
>and annoy the heck out of you (which, if I'm really a jerk, would be
>my real goal -- the $500 is just salt in the wound).
>--Tim Smith

Even if you "register the copyright" it does not prevent the recipient
of your letter from showing that letter to anyone else he/she chooses.
Even if you sent it via email, the recipient could print out a copy to
have a hard copy and that would fall in the fair use exceptions of
the copyright law.

As has been well stated before by others in this newsgroup, the recipient
of a letter owns that copy of the letter (note I did not say the recipient
owns the copyright nor that the recipient can make multiple copies of the 
letter).  As the owner of the letter, the recipient can do anything with it
regardless of any statement you make in the letter to the contrary.
The only way you could claim your letter should never have been shown to
anyone without your consent would be if you and the recipient had
previously agreed on such non-disclosure.  Generally that is a written
agreement to that affect.  Certainly a verbal non-disclosure is
possible, but unless there was a way to corroborate such a verbal agreement
you'd have a tough time claiming there was one after the fact.
Also, even with a non-disclosure agreement, the only thing you'd have 
if the recipient disclosed the your letter would be a violation of the
agreement.  You would thus need to have pre agreed to terms of
what, if any, remidies the non-disclosure agreement offered you
if your letter was disclosed.

Note also, that the recipient can, in fact, publish or otherwise report
excerpts from such a letter and be wholly within the legalities of copyright
laws.  For example, if your letter contained a sentance in which you
stated: "John Doe eats worms.", the recipient of your letter could certainly
post that statement in a newsgroup or report it in a newspaper article
by saying:  In a letter I recieved from Tim Smith, he said "John Doe
eat's worms."  Such a single or otherwise short excerpt from a letter
(or any other published material) is also covered by the fair use exceptions
of US copyright law.

