This refers to the woman's secondary sex characteristics (bust).  It
is usually considered poor ettiquette to refer to anyone's bust in a
work setting, which a university is.

Refering to the young fellow as "fine" (which also means good) and
"young", both of which are considered positive attributes in our
society, the poem subtly suggests that the woman is bad, or at least
damns her with faint praise, relative to the references to the man.

Referring to the woman's body explicitly and not to the man's
reinforces the standard that it is OK to objectify a woman in a way
that it is not OK to objectify a man.

The poem's structure suggests that the lust is the woman's, again
putting the man and woman on an uneven footing.

"Years of struggle, coffin, dust" suggests that marriage is a bad
deal, and "Handsome woman, lovely bust" can be construed as implying
that the man was lured into that bad deal by the woman.

The points after the first are all more a matter of perception than
the first point, which stands without any of the others.

